{"type":"standard","title":"Euclidean distance","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Euclidean distance</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q847073","titles":{"canonical":"Euclidean_distance","normalized":"Euclidean distance","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Euclidean distance</span></span>"},"pageid":53932,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Euclidean_distance_2d.svg/330px-Euclidean_distance_2d.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","width":330,"height":227},"originalimage":{"source":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Euclidean_distance_2d.svg/500px-Euclidean_distance_2d.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","width":360,"height":248},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1375088743","tid":"1ea12d9f-b143-11f1-8586-3ab80ca9dbb6","timestamp":"2026-09-15T20:22:06Z","description":"Length of a line segment","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_distance","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_distance?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_distance?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Euclidean_distance"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_distance","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Euclidean_distance","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_distance?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Euclidean_distance"}},"extract":"In mathematics, the Euclidean distance between two points in a Euclidean space is the length of the line segment between them. It can be calculated from the Cartesian coordinates of the points using the Pythagorean theorem, and therefore is occasionally called the Pythagorean distance.","extract_html":"<p>In mathematics, the <b>Euclidean distance</b> between two points in a Euclidean space is the length of the line segment between them. It can be calculated from the Cartesian coordinates of the points using the Pythagorean theorem, and therefore is occasionally called the <b>Pythagorean distance</b>.</p>"}